From d4b9583147fe8b15b284f4fbbfd9fe2b02723fec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Rapha=C3=ABl=20M=C3=A9lotte?= <raphael.melotte@mind.be>
Date: Mon, 27 Jul 2026 12:41:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] scripts/wrap/parse.py: get_members(): test cursor.kind, not
 cursor.type.kind.
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

get_members() resolves a typedef to its underlying type's declaration
before looking at its children:

    if cursor.type.kind in (TypeKind.TYPEDEF, TypeKind.ELABORATED):
        cursor2 = cursor.underlying_typedef_type.get_declaration()

The intent seems to be "if <cursor> is itself a typedef declaration,
follow it to the real declaration", but the condition instead tests
the *type* of the entity the cursor declares. Those are not the same
thing: a FIELD_DECL cursor for a plain data member can have a type
whose .kind is ELABORATED - this is normal for a field whose type is
an anonymous struct/union defined inline, e.g. `union { struct {...}
t; ...} u;`. The old condition therefore matched such fields too, and
went on to call `.underlying_typedef_type` on a cursor that is not a
TYPEDEF_DECL at all.

clang_getTypedefDeclUnderlyingType() is documented to return an
invalid type if the cursor does not reference a typedef
declaration (clang/include/clang-c/Index.h) - but the result for a
field cursor is unusable: on clang 18, the invalid type's
.get_declaration() comes back as a NO_DECL_FOUND cursor with no
location, whose .get_children() is unconditionally empty.
get_members() falls through to its type_.get_fields() fallback in that
case, which happens to still find the real members, so this was
previously masked rather than fixed.

On clang 22, this same call is not reached in the first place: clang
22 removed ElaboratedType from the AST (see the "Clang Python Bindings
Potentially Breaking Changes" entry in the Clang 22.1.0 release notes:
"TypeKind ELABORATED is not used anymore, per clang AST changes
removing ElaboratedTypes"), so a field whose type is an anonymous
struct/union now reports TypeKind.RECORD instead of
TypeKind.ELABORATED, and the old condition is simply false. This
commit should therefore be needed only for clang<22.1.0.

Fix by checking cursor.kind == CursorKind.TYPEDEF_DECL, which matches
only genuine typedef declarations (e.g.  `typedef struct fz_pixmap
fz_pixmap;`) and leaves plain fields - whose type merely happens to be
elaborated/anonymous - to fall through to direct child inspection,
which is what actually finds their members.

Combined with the next commit (which makes get_members() descend into
the placeholder cursor libclang gives us for an anonymous struct/union
field), this makes get_members() resolve such fields the same way, for
the same reason, on both clang 18 and clang 22, rather than clang 18
only working by accident.

Upstream: https://bugs.ghostscript.com/show_bug.cgi?id=709448
Signed-off-by: Raphaël Mélotte <raphael.melotte@mind.be>
---
 scripts/wrap/parse.py | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/scripts/wrap/parse.py b/scripts/wrap/parse.py
index ed1737b34..8881c68d0 100644
--- a/scripts/wrap/parse.py
+++ b/scripts/wrap/parse.py
@@ -116,7 +116,7 @@ def get_members( type_or_cursor, include_empty=False):
         cursor = type_or_cursor
     else:
         assert 0
-    if cursor.type.kind in (state.clang.cindex.TypeKind.TYPEDEF, state.clang.cindex.TypeKind.ELABORATED):
+    if cursor.kind == state.clang.cindex.CursorKind.TYPEDEF_DECL:
         cursor2 = cursor.underlying_typedef_type.get_declaration()
     else:
         cursor2 = cursor
-- 
2.54.0

